{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1583749884/10/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"409aa875\" lines=\"68-81\">\n<description>\nFire projectiles from 3-pixel triangle sprites; mark collisions with maroon or blue.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Non-bg 3-pixel sprites shaped as axis-aligned L's or diagonal V's.\n\nRule:\n- Each triangle has an apex and an emission direction determined by its shape: L-shapes fire diagonally away from the missing corner; V-shapes fire along the axis away from the open side.\n- A projectile lands exactly 5 cells from the apex in the emission direction.\n- If it hits an existing sprite, that entire sprite is recolored to maroon (9).\n- If two projectiles land on the same empty cell, that cell becomes blue (1); a lone projectile on empty bg paints maroon (9).\n\nKey insight: The triangle's orientation encodes both direction and target; dual-projectile collisions on open bg produce blue, not maroon.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e87109e9\" lines=\"68-83\">\n<description>\nEmit four teal beams from a square, turning at colored obstacles per a legend.\n\nInput structure:\n- Top band: grey (5) legend with colored vertical bars in cells; bar position (left or right) encodes turn direction.\n- Below: main grid with bg, a solid teal (8) square, and colored obstacle blocks.\n\nRule:\n- Remove the legend from the output.\n- From the teal square, shoot a beam in each cardinal direction (as a sliding copy of the square).\n- Each beam paints teal over bg cells as it moves.\n- On hitting an obstacle of color C, consult the legend: bar on left → turn counterclockwise, bar on right → turn clockwise. Continue in the new direction.\n- Beams stop at grid edges.\n\nKey insight: The legend's bar position (left vs right within its cell) is the encoding for turn direction at each obstacle color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"48634b99\" lines=\"14-27\">\n<description>\nAdvance the marker from one half of a vertical bar to the same half of the next longer bar.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has one dominant background color and exactly two foreground colors.\n- One foreground color forms many vertical one-cell-wide bars; the other appears as a single vertical marker segment.\n- The marker segment occupies exactly the top or bottom half of its containing vertical bar.\nRule\n- Recolor the current marker segment back to the bar color.\n- Find the unique all-bar-color vertical bar whose height is two pixels greater than the marked source bar.\n- Recolor the same half of that next longer bar to the marker color.\nKey insight\n- Each bar's total height encodes an ordered size, and the marker advances from a 2k bar to the corresponding 2(k+1) bar while preserving whether it marks the top or bottom half.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7666fa5d\" lines=\"12-26\">\n<description>\nFill the parallelogram-shaped region between parallel anti-diagonal line segments with red.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- One non-bg color forms short diagonal segments, each lying on a single anti-diagonal (r+c = const).\n- Segments come in parallel pairs bounding a region to be filled.\n\nRule:\n- For each main-diagonal axis (r−c = const), collect anti-diagonal segments whose span covers that axis.\n- Sort those segments by their anti-diagonal index (r+c).\n- Fill all bg cells lying strictly between consecutive segment pairs along that axis with red (color 2).\n\nKey insight: The two diagonal directions decouple — segments sit on anti-diagonals, and filling sweeps along main diagonals between them, forming parallelogram regions.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"afe3afe9\" lines=\"106-119\">\n<description>\nConvert each hollow 3x3 symbol to one pixel and slide the pixels clockwise from the marked edge.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a background, one full-edge marker line, and monochrome hollow 3x3 foreground symbols.\n- The symbols lie on a coarse row/column lattice; fully empty lattice rows or columns are absent from the output.\n\nRule\n- Replace each hollow 3x3 symbol with one pixel of its color in the corresponding coarse-grid cell.\n- The marker edge gives the compaction direction by clockwise border travel: top -> right, right -> down, bottom -> left, left -> up.\n- Preserve the order of non-background pixels while sliding them as far as possible in that direction.\n\nKey insight\n- The large picture is a scaled symbolic grid, and the blue edge marker acts as a gravity direction rather than as output content.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"fe45cba4\" lines=\"72-89\">\n<description>\nMove the left-edge fragment into its same-colored anchor fragment to complete a solid rectangle.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has a dominant background color.\n- Exactly one foreground color is split into two 4-connected objects: one\n  touches the left edge, and the other is an anchor away from the left edge.\n- The translated left-edge object and the anchor can form one filled rectangle.\nRule\n- Find the unique split color whose left-edge object can be translated onto\n  background cells beside the other same-colored object so their union is a\n  solid axis-aligned rectangle.\n- Clear the left-edge object's original cells to the background.\n- Paint that object at the unique completing offset, preserving all other cells.\nKey insight\n- The active color is identified by object structure and rectangle completion,\n  not by a fixed color id; the same-colored anchor object stays in place.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"71e489b6\" lines=\"7-19\">\n<description>\nHighlight black defects in blue foreground by surrounding them with orange.\n\nInput structure:\n- Blue (1) foreground on black (0) bg; small black holes or protrusions within blue regions; occasional thin blue spurs into black areas.\n\nRule:\n- Thin blue pixels mostly surrounded by black are absorbed into the background.\n- Small black components (≤3 cells) and leaf pixels of larger black components are \"defects.\"\n- Each defect gets an orange (7) 8-neighbor halo; the defect pixels themselves stay black.\n\nKey insight: Only structural anomalies—tiny isolated patches and dangling endpoints—are marked, not bulk black regions.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"af726779\" lines=\"17-35\">\n<description>\nGrow an alternating pink/orange triangular trace downward from the horizontal orange seed row.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has a green background and one horizontal row containing all orange foreground pixels.\n- The seed row may contain isolated orange pixels and adjacent orange runs.\n- Pink is absent from the input and is used for the first generated row.\n\nRule:\n- Copy the input grid.\n- Two rows below the current active row, place a child pixel between each active pair\n  whose middle cell in the current row is background.\n- Alternate generated row colors between pink and orange, stopping when no children\n  can be generated or the next row would leave the grid.\n\nKey insight:\n- Only an active-background-active triplet in the current row reproduces; contiguous\n  active runs do not produce children through occupied middle cells.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e45ef808\" lines=\"31-46\">\n<description>\nMark the shallowest and deepest columns of a solid terrain boundary with vertical colored guide lines.\n\nInput structure:\n- The first row is a solid empty separator.\n- Below it, each column consists of upper fill cells followed by a contiguous suffix of terrain cells.\n- The bottom row is all terrain, so every column has a well-defined first terrain row.\n\nRule:\n- Find the column whose first terrain row is latest; it is unique and its upper fill run becomes maroon.\n- Find the rightmost column whose first terrain row is earliest; its upper fill run becomes yellow.\n- Preserve the empty separator, terrain cells, and all other upper fill cells.\n\nKey insight:\n- The task is a column-height summary of the terrain profile: color the deepest upper run and the highest terrain peak.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"22208ba4\" lines=\"12-27\">\n<description>\nMove the repeated boundary color's rectangles one block inward while leaving singleton boundary rectangles in place.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the most common color.\n- Foreground objects are solid monochrome rectangles touching a grid corner.\n- Exactly one foreground color appears as multiple separate 4-connected objects; other colors appear once.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the multiple objects of the repeated color from their original positions.\n- For each cleared rectangle, translate it away from the row and column border it touches by exactly its height and width.\n- Keep singleton-color objects unchanged; overlapping translated rectangles of the same color merge.\n\nKey insight:\n- Multiplicity selects the moving color, and each boundary rectangle's touched edges determine its inward diagonal translation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"cb2d8a2c\" lines=\"13-26\">\n<description>\nExtend a green path from one grid edge to the opposite, routing around red bars at a distance dictated by their blue pixel count.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = azure (8). Red/blue bars (color 2/1) act as obstacles.\n- A single green (3) pixel sits on a grid border — the path start.\n\nRule:\n- Each bar's keep-out radius equals its number of blue pixels; the path must stay farther than that from every bar cell.\n- The green pixel extends inward toward the opposite edge, turning at right angles to navigate around forbidden zones.\n- After the path is drawn, all blue pixels become red.\n\nKey insight: Blue pixels on a bar encode its danger radius — more blue means a wider no-go zone the path must respect.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"80214e03\" lines=\"48-64\">\n<description>\nExtract the color of each noisy block in a row/column lattice and mirror the coarse grid horizontally.\n\nInput structure:\n- A background surrounds a rectangular lattice of monochrome foreground blocks.\n- Consecutive rows in a lattice band share the same set of visible foreground colors.\n- Consecutive columns in a lattice band share the same set of visible foreground colors.\n- Each row-band/column-band intersection has one foreground color, with possible background holes.\n\nRule:\n- Group non-empty rows and columns into consecutive bands by their foreground color sets.\n- Replace each lattice cell by the single foreground color inside that row/column-band intersection.\n- Return the resulting coarse color grid with the column order reversed.\n\nKey insight:\n- Whole-row and whole-column color sets reveal the hidden lattice even when neighboring cells share a color or contain holes.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"ff2825db\" lines=\"18-36\">\n<description>\nSelect the majority interior marker color, then redraw the framed board as that color's frame plus its bounding-box outline.\n\nInput structure:\n- The top row is a copied palette made from adjacent equal color pairs.\n- Below it is a one-cell-thick rectangular frame around a black interior.\n- Non-black, non-frame interior pixels are marker colors; one marker color has\n  the unique highest count.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the palette row unchanged.\n- Pick the uniquely most frequent interior marker color.\n- Clear the lower board to black, recolor its outer frame with the picked color,\n  and draw the picked color's interior bounding-box border.\n\nKey insight:\n- The noisy marker cloud is not preserved as pixels; only the majority marker\n  color and its spatial extent define the output.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"195c6913\" lines=\"37-52\">\n<description>\nSimulate particles along a path with alternating turn direction at collisions.\n\nInput structure:\n- Top-left: 2x2 monochrome blocks define a repeating color cycle (left-to-right).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
