ed regions in the direction dictated by border-legend arrows.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/88e364bc.py:169:\"\"\"Generator for task 88e364bc (slide yellow in walled regions by legend arrows).\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:11:# In the input, you will see several objects of the same color that are in an arrowhead shape and facing different directions.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:12:# The goal is to find the directions of the arrowheads and draw lines that would represent the path they had been moving in to go in that direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:16:    Extend each small arrowhead with a line in the direction it points.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:19:    - The input is a black grid with several same-colored arrowhead objects.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:20:    - Each arrowhead is a small shape whose missing corner indicates its direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:21:    - Lines should use the arrowhead color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:24:    - Crop each arrowhead and compare the mean positions of colored and black cells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:26:    - Draw a line from the matching outer corner of the arrowhead's bounding box to the grid edge.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:29:    - The arrowhead asymmetry identifies both the direction of travel and the corner where the path begins.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:37:    # for each object, find the direction the arrowhead is pointing in by finding the relative mean position of colored and black pixels in the bounding box of the object\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:60:        # find the direction the arrowhead is pointing in, it is from the mean position of the colored pixels to the mean position of the black pixels\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:63:        # draw a line in the direction the arrowhead is pointing in from the corresponding corner of the bounding box\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:70:            # check if the corner is in the direction that the arrowhead is pointing\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:81:    # choose the color of the arrowheads\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:84:    # make the arrowheads by making a 2x2 square then removing one of the corners randomly and placing it randomly on the grid\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:86:        # make at arrowhead\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:87:        arrowhead = np.full((2, 2), color)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:89:        arrowhead[corner // 2, corner % 2] = Color.BLACK\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:92:        _loc = first_free_location(grid, arrowhead, transparent=Color.BLACK, border=0, padding=1, padding_connectivity=8)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/6e19193c.py:96:        paste(grid, arrowhead, r, c, transparent=Color.BLACK, clip=True)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC1_code/train/0a938d79.py:81:    # make a grid that is narrow and tall. This is for horizontal lines, and we will flip a coin at the end to transpose everything to make things vertical\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/689c358e.py:73:    Mark each arrow-like sprite's pointed frame endpoint with its color and the opposite endpoint black.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/689c358e.py:77:    - Inside the frame are monochrome six-pixel sprites shaped as asymmetric T arrows.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/7b80bb43/8/code.py:28:#    The topology family is still narrow enough that 2-4 examples support the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9385bd28/7/code.py:248:    - Neither color alone nor shape frequency alone is sufficient here. Color narrows the search, shape identifies which\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/31/code.py:19:#    direction. This combo narrows that geometry to one specific family: a 1-pixel rectangular\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/edb79dae/11/code.py:525:# The generator keeps the original global geometry family but narrows some degrees of freedom so the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/4/code.py:782:def _place_docked_trains(base, bg, regions, label_pool, arrow_color, n_trains, shared_size=None):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/4/code.py:813:                output[indicator_abs] = arrow_color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/4/code.py:913:    arrow_color = random.choice(extra_pool)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/4/code.py:914:    label_pool = [c for c in extra_pool if c != arrow_color]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/4/code.py:924:        arrow_color,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/4/code.py:932:        t[\"indicator_color\"] = arrow_color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e4f6532/4/code.py:934:    metrics = _combo_metrics(trains, arrow_color)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9385bd28/6/code.py:44:#    objects inside a narrow interior activity strip aligned with the long grid\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9385bd28/6/code.py:68:#    Active objects share one narrow strip but do not overlap after expansion.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/37/code.py:31:#    period (AABB). This keeps the rule rich enough to be seen but narrow enough\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d8e07eb2/20/code.py:165:    The answer is not found by global glyph identity. Color only narrows the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9385bd28/12/code.py:129:    ambiguity in narrow bounding boxes.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/18/code.py:27:#        narrowed interval, stop before painting the stripe;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/18/code.py:30:#      - gap line: if the original input has foreground inside the current narrowed interval,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/32/code.py:28:#      k-th outward depth layer remains visible, where k is 2 for narrow/medium\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/edb79dae/28/code.py:23:#    output shows a large silhouette with a narrow smaller-color trace inside the overlap.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/17/code.py:72:    Key insight: The endpoints no longer define immediate full-width radiation; they identify a narrow outlet, and the exterior fill must respect a fixed bottleneck width first, then a gradually widening cone.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/31f7f899/8/code.py:53:     width. After narrowing the baseline, a tall bar can rival that count. Fix: detect\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/3/code.py:9:#   inside the original segment span. Originally, yellow stripes are narrow span-only\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/3/code.py:10:#   marks, while green stripes are narrow but also flood the full row/column.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/3/code.py:191:    - yellow stripes keep the original narrow segment span but flood the full row/col\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2d0172a1/28/code.py:29:#    2x4 horizontal arrow, a near-square inner frame, and a 4x2 vertical arrow, the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e87109e9/21/code.py:37:#    The obstacle family stays narrow and readable: 3-4 solid edge bars, 2-4 cell\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e87109e9/21/code.py:162:    arrow_patch = mask[_ARROW_ROWS, _ARROW_COLS]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e87109e9/21/code.py:164:        if np.array_equal(arrow_patch, template):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e87109e9/21/code.py:166:    raise AssertionError(f\"unrecognized legend arrow patch:\\n{arrow_patch.astype(int)}\")\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e87109e9/21/code.py:292:    \"\"\"Build the fixed 6x24 legend band using swatches plus mirrored elbow arrows.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e87109e9/21/code.py:572:    Trace legend-ordered teal rays whose turn rules come from arrow glyphs.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e87109e9/21/code.py:595:    arrow glyph gives each obstacle color's turn direction, and the visible\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:46:#    replaces that with one yellow edge-L arrow plus one colored corner marker.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:47:#    The arrow has two perpendicular arms that already encode an ordered pair of candidate\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:48:#    directions: the longer arm is the arrow's direct direction, and the shorter arm is exactly\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:49:#    the clockwise-rotated alternative. Example: a top-edge arrow with elbow at (0, 6), long\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:55:#    on row 0 and later stripes appear on rows 2, 4, ... . The source arrow therefore matters\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:59:#    visible lengths and the longer arm is forced to be unique. That keeps the \"arrow points by\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:68:#      direction. Now the edge only says \"the arrow is on this side\"; the actual direction comes\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:73:#    - exactly one salient yellow component is the arrow source\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:79:#    The generator chooses one of eight edge templates so the arrow fits fully in-bounds, keeps\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:81:#    corner marker only after the arrow is fixed. Interior blobs are placed with padded free\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:82:#    placement on a canvas that already contains the arrow and marker, so blobs cannot overlap\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:86:#    starting source is now visibly an arrow-like L rather than a plain segment. Comparing a few\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:88:#    arrow's long arm or its clockwise-rotated shorter arm. Because the two candidate outputs\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:92:#    `main()` assumes `_extract_arrow()` finds a single largest yellow L and that its shorter arm\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:98:#    contiguous row-span or col-span; the generator's arrow is made from straight runs, so that\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:145:def _extract_arrow(g: np.ndarray) -> dict[str, object] | None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:202:    arrow: dict[str, object],\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:208:    elbow_r, elbow_c = (int(v) for v in arrow[\"elbow\"])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:211:        move_dir = str(arrow[\"rot_dir\"])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:212:        span_arm = list(arrow[\"base_arm\"])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:214:        move_dir = str(arrow[\"base_dir\"])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:215:        span_arm = list(arrow[\"rot_arm\"])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:283:    arrow = _extract_arrow(g)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:284:    if arrow is None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:286:    return _paint_pattern(g, bg, _layout(arrow, g.shape, rotated=rotated))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:291:    Propagate the original Y,Y,G stripe process, but decode direction from a yellow edge-L arrow\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:321:    arrow = _extract_arrow(g)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:322:    if arrow is None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:331:    actual_layout = _layout(arrow, g.shape, rotated=rotated)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:333:        \"base_dir\": str(arrow[\"base_dir\"]),\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:334:        \"rot_dir\": str(arrow[\"rot_dir\"]),\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:336:        \"base_len\": int(arrow[\"base_len\"]),\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:337:        \"rot_len\": int(arrow[\"rot_len\"]),\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:345:def _paint_arrow(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:363:def _sample_arrow_spec(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:454:    spec = _sample_arrow_spec(rng, H, W)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:460:    _paint_arrow(grid, elbow=elbow, base_dir=base_dir, base_len=base_len, rot_len=rot_len)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:521:    arrow = _extract_arrow(canvas)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:522:    if arrow is None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/11/code.py:525:    layout = _layout(arrow, canvas.shape, rotated=rotated)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:36:#             and carve a virtual breach through the capped side selected by the arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:43:#        and places the outside arrow one gap away from the mouth side.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:49:#      separate from the outside arrow and from sparse obstacles.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:50:#    - The outside arrow has only four possible directions and always uses one 2-pixel\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:69:#      obstacle. That would incorrectly make the external arrow cast shadows, so the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:73:#      arrow first and then score the trigger's 4-neighbors by dot product with that\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:74:#      arrow direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:93:#      for where the exterior radiation should go is the detached 2-pixel arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:94:#    - Across examples the same wall family appears with different arrow directions\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:99:#    - The two markers live in different zones: trigger on the wall, arrow outside\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:102:#      that could be mistaken for the outside arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:118:#      distances from its two pixels; generator makes that the only external arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:121:#    - The trigger has at least one passable 4-neighbor in the arrow direction and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:332:    - Treat the trigger as part of the wall, decode the arrow to decide which capped side becomes a virtual opening, and start flood-fill from the trigger's inner-side neighbor.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:335:    Key insight: Neither old cue survives on its own: the fill source is no longer inside the enclosure, and the opening is no longer visible in the wall shape, so the solver must combine the border trigger with the detached arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:436:    assert best_score > 0, \"expected trigger interior to lie in the arrow-selected direction\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:635:    dist_arrow = distance_transform_cdt(~dir_marker_mask, metric=\"chessboard\")\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/15/code.py:636:    eligible = (grid == bg) & (dist_solid > 1) & (dist_arrow > 1)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b9e38dc0/38/code.py:381:    - An outer-arc gap emits a narrow band toward the right side of the grid, while a bridge gap emits a vertical band toward the nearest border; each band is bounded by rays launched from the two wall tips around that gap.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/edb79dae/6/code.py:10:#    - Combo: the frame interior is blank. Instead, single-cell markers sit in a narrow band left of\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/edb79dae/6/code.py:153:    - Just outside the frame, a narrow band on the left carries colored row markers and a narrow band\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/31f7f899/2/code.py:67:       centers narrower bars inside it, so any shape can legally move to any slot.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e87109e9/36/code.py:10:      - replace each legend bar with a bottom-aligned colored hook / arrow token inside a 4x4 black cell\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/5/code.py:257:    - A human sees three repeating colors inside the same narrow span: yellow stripe, red separator,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/33/code.py:36:#    The cascade is intentionally narrow: one ordered chain, one visible comparison key per step, and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/12/code.py:11:# - D10/1 narrows the span by 1 on every stripe. With a top segment spanning\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/12/code.py:22:#   This yields a finite wide-to-narrow cone rather than painting to the far edge.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/221dfab4/12/code.py:66:#   * the stripe cone narrows as it advances,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/cbebaa4b/17/code.py:32:#     - tabs are narrower than the strip, which makes concave/convex shoulders visible without exploding the case count\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e376de54/21/code.py:29:#    The added freedom is intentionally narrow. There is still one shared direction, 3 or 5 segments, and one median\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e376de54/30/code.py:56:#    Gap sizes remain in a narrow range so the board stays sparse and legible.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:31:    - each control is now a 3-cell L-shaped arrow touching exactly one border:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:39:      arrow's border position encodes one of three visible depth bins by distance\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:45:        top arrow at column 8 on a 20-wide grid is near the top-edge midpoint,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:46:        so after decoding a downward arrow the solver draws a long vertical ray on\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:47:        column 8. A top arrow at column 7 is farther from the midpoint, so the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:52:  - I keep only one extra shape family: four cardinal arrow orientations. That\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:63:    one arrow would look like three controls. New solver first extracts 4-connected\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:72:    the full 3-cell arrow must be preserved in output.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:75:    come from decoded arrow launches.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:79:    `[1, H-2]`, so every arrow touches exactly one border.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:81:    arrows never overlap fg.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:89:  - A human can see arrow orientation directly from the 3-cell corner shape and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:104:  - All ctrl cells belong to 1 or 2 size-3 L-shaped arrow components.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:146:def _decode_arrow(comp: list[tuple[int, int]], H: int, W: int) -> dict[str, object]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:182:        raise AssertionError(\"Unexpected control arrow shape\")\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:187:def _find_arrows(g: np.ndarray, ctrl: int) -> list[dict[str, object]]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:188:    arrows = [_decode_arrow(comp, *g.shape) for comp in _components4(g == int(ctrl))]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:189:    arrows.sort(key=lambda a: (str(a[\"edge\"]), tuple(a[\"launch\"])))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:190:    return arrows\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:320:    arrows = _find_arrows(g, ctrl)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:324:    for arrow in arrows:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:325:        edge = str(arrow[\"edge\"])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:326:        launch_r, launch_c = tuple(int(x) for x in arrow[\"launch\"])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:336:    for arrow in arrows:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:340:            str(arrow[\"edge\"]),\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:341:            tuple(int(x) for x in arrow[\"launch\"]),\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:348:    for arrow in arrows:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:349:        for r, c in tuple(arrow[\"cells\"]):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:395:    Decode each border arrow, use its orientation as ray direction and its border\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:426:def _make_arrow(edge: str, split: int, H: int, W: int) -> tuple[set[tuple[int, int]], tuple[int, int]]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:467:def _count_nonlong_arrows(arrows: list[dict[str, object]], H: int, W: int) -> int:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:469:    for arrow in arrows:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:470:        launch_r, launch_c = tuple(int(x) for x in arrow[\"launch\"])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:471:        edge = str(arrow[\"edge\"])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:494:    arrows: list[dict[str, object]] = []\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:504:            cells, launch = _make_arrow(edge, int(c_split), H, W)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:505:            arrows.append({\"edge\": edge, \"cells\": cells, \"launch\": launch})\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:511:            cells, launch = _make_arrow(edge, int(r_split), H, W)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:512:            arrows.append({\"edge\": edge, \"cells\": cells, \"launch\": launch})\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:745:    for arrow in arrows:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:746:        for r, c in set(arrow[\"cells\"]):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:749:    if _count_nonlong_arrows(arrows, H, W) < 1:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:778:    arrows = _find_arrows(g, ctrl)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:780:    nonlong_controls = int(_count_nonlong_arrows(arrows, H, W))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:783:    for arrow in arrows:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:784:        edge = str(arrow[\"edge\"])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/37/code.py:785:        launch = tuple(int(x) for x in arrow[\"launch\"])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:15:#   a 2-pixel arrow stub made from the transferable payload color,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:19:#   source.arrow -> dominant-axis direction to the target destination,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:32:#   in the encoded arrow direction. So arrow direction alone is insufficient.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:34:#   corners compatible with the chosen arrow direction. This keeps the symbolic alphabet small.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:42:#   that border class and then resolve the target by arrow direction plus marker quadrant.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:43:# - The D15-style directional cue is compressed to a 2-pixel center+neighbor arrow stub. That\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:46:#   3-pixel arrow while remaining a single readable object.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:51:# - Sources are always fully visible 3x3 boxes so both the arrow and the diagonal marker are\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:53:# - Source placement is filtered so the two destinations are both in the source's arrow direction\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:58:# - The input visibly contains two deleted source boxes, each with an arrow and an extra corner\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:65:# - The arrow is a compact 2-pixel center+edge pointer, while the marker remains a single corner\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:75:# - For each real source, both destinations share the arrow direction but occupy the two marker\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:152:    assert patch[mr, mc] == border_color, \"marker corner must not overlap the arrow\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:409:def _detect_arrow_marker_sources(g: np.ndarray, bg: int, grey: int) -> list[dict]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:412:    The payload forms one of four 2-pixel arrow stubs and includes the center cell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:413:    The marker is a singleton corner not occupied by the arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:523:    - Each real source box has three simultaneous parts: a shared border color, a 2-pixel payload-colored arrow stub, and one extra diagonal marker pixel in a corner.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:529:    - Among those same-border candidates, keep the unique source whose arrow points toward the destination and whose diagonal marker corner matches the destination's quadrant relative to that source.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:533:    - Matching is now layered: the destination center color selects a source border class, the arrow gives a coarse global direction, and the diagonal marker resolves which destination inside that direction cone is the true target.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:540:    sources = _detect_arrow_marker_sources(g, bg, grey)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:542:    assert sources, \"expected marked arrow sources\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/d35bdbdc/17/code.py:770:    sources = _detect_arrow_marker_sources(g, bg, GREY)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/23/code.py:61:    Detect embedded '+' motifs inside a narrow strip and recolor each one to teal.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/1818057f/23/code.py:66:    - The true '+' motifs are arranged on one shared row or one shared column, so they read as a narrow 1-D chain, while other foreground cells are only distractors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f3a5a89/16/code.py:26:#          carve long narrow chambers rather than roughly square rooms.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:20:# - The head is no longer a full edge line. It is a fixed 3x3 arrow sprite:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:26:#   in the arrow's pointing direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:27:# - The translation distance is measured from the arrow tip to the point, not from arrow bbox\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:28:#   top-left and not from a full line center. After moving k steps, the arrow tip advances by k and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:33:#   D4/2 alone: an arrow attached on the left may still point down, so direction comes from shape.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:35:#   Combo here: an arrow attached orthogonally to the motion axis points toward a single dot, and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:36:#   the arrow tip rather than any bbox anchor is what aligns to that dot.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:37:#   Example: a right-pointing arrow attached above the container has tip at (r, c). A point sits at\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:42:# - The arrow vocabulary is still only four shapes, keeping shape parsing low-entropy.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:43:# - To ensure this is visibly different from the source task, the generator only attaches arrows on\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:46:# - The target is always a point with at least one background cell between it and the arrow tip, so\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:51:#   Broken. In the combo, the arrow may be above the box while motion is right.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:55:#   top-left. Broken again: a point has no meaningful shared shape anchor with the arrow. The new\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:56:#   anchor is the arrow tip.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:58:#   between arrow tip and point. The solver still contains a tiny fallback for malformed gap=0\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:59:#   inputs by trying to peel one extra pixel off an otherwise valid arrow component.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:62:# - Each generated assembly reserves the union of five regions: input container, input arrow,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:63:#   destination point, moved container, moved arrow. Packing is done with a 1-cell background moat.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:68:# - Across samples, the arrow is visibly not on the side of travel, forcing the solver to read its\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:73:# - Only three foreground object types appear: bordered container, arrow head, point target.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:74:#   The point is sparse, the arrow is compact, and assemblies are separated by background.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:78:#   direction, so the only new inference burden is the fused geometry: arrow orientation + tip to\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:82:# - Head components are exact 3x3 arrow sprites, or that sprite plus one fused forward point in the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:84:# - Every mover has exactly one nearest forward point on its arrow ray; the generator verifies\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:86:# - Motion direction, fill order, and point alignment are all keyed off the arrow direction, not\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:147:def _arrow_tl_relative(outer_h: int, outer_w: int, attach_side: str, anchor: int) -> tuple[int, int]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:157:def _stamp_arrow(grid: np.ndarray, tl: tuple[int, int], direction: str, color: int) -> None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:180:def _parse_arrow_component(pts: set[tuple[int, int]]) -> dict | None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:226:    - The source marker is a 3x3 arrow sprite whose orientation gives the movement direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:228:    - Distance is measured from the arrow tip to the target point.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:290:        parsed = _parse_arrow_component(set(comp0[\"pts\"]))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:422:        head_r, head_c = _arrow_tl_relative(outer_h, outer_w, attach_side, anchor)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:531:        _stamp_arrow(grid, (hr, hc), s[\"direction\"], HEAD_COLOR)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/7/code.py:563:        _stamp_arrow(expected, (nhr, nhc), s[\"direction\"], HEAD_COLOR)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f3a5a89/9/code.py:36:#      component, orthogonal rooms, and a small number of narrow chokepoints.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f3a5a89/9/code.py:48:#      narrow doorway would be mistaken for a real passage and the maze would\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:23:#    - D3/0: the external source object becomes an arrow sprite with one ring tip\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:27:#    Example: an arrow touching the top ring at column c points downward into the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:29:#    entire interior column from top to bottom. If the arrow is two-colored, the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:31:#    it. A bottom-touching arrow at the same column would paint the same geometry\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:37:#    - there are 2-3 arrows per grid, not just one\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:38:#    - arrows can start from either of two opposite sides of the chosen axis\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:39:#    - monochrome and two-color arrows both appear\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:40:#    - at least one arrow per sample is two-color so direction is behaviorally\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:43:#    either vertical rays (top/bottom arrows) or horizontal rays (left/right\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:44:#    arrows). That avoids row/column crossings that would otherwise create noisy\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:49:#    - useful components no longer have >=2 ring touches; each arrow has exactly 1\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:52:#    - U-pairs are incompatible with \"one arrow -> one through-line\", so they are\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:55:#    has the local arrow-head geometry for that side, then derives the full line\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:60:#    then converts each line into a single external arrow with:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:71:#    plainly an inward-pointing arrow attached to one side of the frame. The\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:82:#    Colors are structural, not id-based. Monochrome arrows produce solid rays.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:83:#    Two-color arrows alternate along the shaft and continue that alternation in\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:90:#    component has one touch plus an arrow head, and the output is full-span\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:96:#    - that ring cell belongs to a valid arrow-head pattern for one side\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:98:#    - useful arrows do not intersect in the cropped output\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:100:#    per sample, dedicated palette colors, and explicit arrow-shape validation in\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:208:def _has_arrow_tip(coords: set[tuple[int, int]], tip: tuple[int, int], side: str) -> bool:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:298:def _place_arrow_component(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:327:    Reconstruct full-span interior rays from external arrows around a framed rectangle.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:331:    - Useful external components are inward-pointing arrows. Each arrow has exactly one tip cell\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:336:    - Map each arrow tip to the corresponding interior boundary cell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:339:    - Monochrome arrows paint solid rays; two-color arrows alternate starting from the tip color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:342:    - The arrow no longer encodes two endpoints. Its single ring tip fixes the line position, and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:343:      the side/orientation of the arrow determines which full-span straight ray must be drawn.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:382:        if side is None or not _has_arrow_tip(coords, tip, side):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:457:    Every sample contains at least one monochrome arrow and one two-color arrow\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:467:def _arrow_specs(side: str, idx: int, H: int, W: int, r0: int, c0: int, r1: int, c1: int):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:586:        inner, backbone, head = _arrow_specs(side, idx, H, W, r0, c0, r1, c1)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/29/code.py:600:        if not _place_arrow_component(grid, comp_mask, backbone, head, colors, bg):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/26/code.py:82:#   - Human solvers can compare narrow vs wide frames and see that one dot centers\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/25/code.py:29:# - The variant is intentionally narrow rather than fully free-form:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/24/code.py:41:  The fused rule instead gives the narrow upper red frame two dots at its legal width-7\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/24/code.py:44:  frame color narrows candidates, spatial proximity resolves shared colors, and frame width\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/24/code.py:92:  differs between narrow and wide frames.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/28/code.py:48:  - The legend vocabulary is intentionally small: only two 3-cell arrow-like glyphs,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:21:# - Outside the container there is now a 3x3 maroon arrow head plus a matching\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:22:#   3x3 maroon destination arrow further along the travel direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:25:#     2. Use the external arrow only as the movable head sprite and as a visual\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:27:# - To make that visible, the head arrow is always attached on a side orthogonal\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:30:#     * internal orange cells form a right-pointing arrow template\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:31:#     * external arrow head also points right, but it is attached above or below\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:34:#       destination arrow to the right of the source arrow, then move container\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:38:#     * fused: internal orange arrow decides direction, head position is decoupled\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:39:#       from direction, and the head itself is a compact arrow sprite\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:43:# - The internal cue is a fixed 4-cell arrow template, not an arbitrary shape.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:44:# - The external cue is a fixed 3x3 arrow sprite.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:53:#   still keep its identity. Broken: a 3x3 arrow must be tracked as a full shape.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:55:#   must depend on the internal arrow direction instead.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:56:# - New assumption introduced here: movers have a unique internal arrow template.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:60:# - New assumption introduced here: the external arrow direction agrees with the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:61:#   internal arrow direction. Generator guarantees this and solver checks it.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:65:#     input container, input head arrow, destination arrow,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:66:#     moved container, moved head arrow\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:70:#   arrows from appearing between a source head and its own destination.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:76:# - The head arrow points the same way as the internal motif, acting as a second\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:81:# - Each assembly contains one container, one source arrow, one destination arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:83:#   head lands exactly on its destination arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:87:# - Orange growth is structurally tied to the same inferred direction: the arrow\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:92:#   old same-side head as an arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:131:def _arrow_template(inner_h: int, inner_w: int, direction: str) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:156:    base = _arrow_template(inner_h, inner_w, direction)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:185:        template = set(_arrow_template(inner_h, inner_w, direction))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:193:def _arrow_tl_relative(outer_h: int, outer_w: int, attach_side: str, anchor: int) -> tuple[int, int]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:203:def _stamp_arrow(grid: np.ndarray, tl: tuple[int, int], direction: str, color: int) -> None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:233:    - Read direction from the internal orange arrow template.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:234:    - The adjacent external 3x3 arrow is the movable head sprite, but its\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:236:    - Move container + head toward the nearest matching arrow destination along\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:370:    for r, c in _arrow_template(inner_h, inner_w, direction):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:414:        head_r, head_c = _arrow_tl_relative(outer_h, outer_w, attach_side, anchor)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:516:        _stamp_arrow(grid, (hr, hc), spec[\"direction\"], HEAD_COLOR)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:517:        _stamp_arrow(grid, (dr, dc), spec[\"direction\"], HEAD_COLOR)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/21/code.py:551:        _stamp_arrow(expected, (nhr, nhc), spec[\"direction\"], HEAD_COLOR)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/24/code.py:62:     into the same narrow area.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/78332cb0/1/code.py:31:#      the color-choice hypothesis narrow and repeated across the full training set.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/6/code.py:199:def _decode_arrow(comp: list[tuple[int, int]], H: int, W: int) -> dict[str, object]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/6/code.py:234:        raise AssertionError(\"Unexpected arrow shape\")\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/6/code.py:257:        arrow = _decode_arrow(comp, H, W)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/6/code.py:258:        arrow[\"color\"] = int(color)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/6/code.py:259:        controls.append(arrow)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/6/code.py:452:def _make_arrow(edge: str, split: int, H: int, W: int) -> tuple[set[tuple[int, int]], tuple[int, int]]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/6/code.py:524:            cells, launch = _make_arrow(edge, c_split, H, W)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/4a21e3da/6/code.py:528:            cells, launch = _make_arrow(edge, r_split, H, W)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/271d71e2/6/code.py:26:#     The final box is two cells narrower and the surviving interior contains two bg holes.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f931b4a8/1/code.py:42:#     - The unused axis is kept narrow (2-4 cells) so the bbox cue is visible\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dbff022c/36/code.py:21:#    \"any enclosed hole\" logic is therefore narrowed to \"interiors of detected rectangle\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/6/code.py:37:#       frame narrower than the widest one, so the D3 change is always observable.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/6/code.py:58:#     - Humans can see two linked effects: narrow frames have fewer possible dot\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/6/code.py:230:    also contains at least one narrower frame.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/6/code.py:233:    narrower_choices = [w for w in FRAME_WIDTH_CHOICES if w < max_width]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/6/code.py:234:    widths = [max_width, int(rng.choice(narrower_choices))]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/6/code.py:251:    At least one widest frame and one narrower frame receive dots so the variable-width\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/6/code.py:258:    narrower = [i for i, width in enumerate(frame_widths) if int(width) < max_width]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8f215267/6/code.py:262:        int(rng.choice(narrower)),\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dbff022c/20/code.py:38:#   narrow, which guarantees a visible disagreement between pixel-majority and side-vote\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:23:#    - D3/0: each meaningful source is now an arrow sprite, not an open frame\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:25:#    - D4/3: the ray starts at the arrow's touchpoint and continues to the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:27:#    - D5/0: the output keeps the full canvas, so arrows remain visible outside\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:29:#    Example: a top-side arrow has its tip on row r0-1 at column c0+1+k and its\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:30:#    head/shaft extend upward, so the arrow visibly points downward into the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:33:#    If the arrow uses two colors, the tip color is also the first ray color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:37:#    2-3 arrows per sample and commonly uses two-color arrows. To avoid visual\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:39:#    vertical rays (top/bottom arrows) or horizontal rays (left/right arrows).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:45:#      Meaningful arrows have exactly one ring-touching tip.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:51:#    The combo therefore needs a new primitive: detect one arrow tip, infer its\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:56:#    for each ray, then places the matching arrow just outside the corresponding\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:63:#    - arrows visibly replace the old open rectangles\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:64:#    - each arrow points into the frame from the side where the ray starts\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:65:#    - the output keeps the whole scene, so the outside arrow and inside ray are\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:72:#    outline, clearly separated arrows, and uncluttered interior stripes.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:75:#    Monochrome arrows produce solid rays. Two-color arrows alternate along the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:87:#    - Meaningful components must have exactly one ring hit and a valid arrow-tip\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:93:#    - Distractors must not mimic an arrow touching the ring. The generator keeps\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:201:def _has_arrow_tip(coords: set[tuple[int, int]], tip: tuple[int, int], side: str) -> bool:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:256:def _decode_arrow_components(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:287:        if side is None or not _has_arrow_tip(coords, tip, side):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:360:def _place_arrow_component(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:387:def _arrow_backbone(side: str, tip: tuple[int, int], length: int) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:487:    Reconstruct full-span interior rays from external arrows while preserving the full canvas.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:491:    - Useful external components are arrow sprites whose tips touch the ring just outside the frame.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:496:    - Reset the frame interior to its fill color, then decode each valid arrow tip as one boundary startpoint.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:498:    - Monochrome arrows produce solid rays; two-color arrows alternate starting from the visible tip color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:502:      \"the opposite side\" rather than \"another touchpoint\", solving reduces to detecting the arrow tip,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:515:    decoded = _decode_arrow_components(g, bg=bg, bc=bc, fill=fill, r0=r0, c0=c0, r1=r1, c1=c1)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:624:        backbone = _arrow_backbone(side, tip, _ARROW_LEN)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/40/code.py:626:        if not _place_arrow_component(grid, comp_mask, backbone, head, colors, bg):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f931b4a8/3/code.py:37:#      narrow and edge-anchored, so the offset cue is visually clean instead of looking like random scatter.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8b7bacbf/25/code.py:20:#      * The same seed line also touches a U-shaped pocket whose 5-region reaches the top edge through a narrow\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/22/code.py:30:   - The generator keeps the path family narrow: two stripe paths come from a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/22/code.py:32:   - The generator keeps the exterior routing narrow and template-based, so\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/bf45cf4b/15/code.py:55:# - The rule space is intentionally narrow: exactly one component, one mask, and exactly 2 rounds.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:25:#    - The useful outer component is now a bent arrow, not an open rectangle.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:27:#    - The arrow's pointing side is read from the tip orientation:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:28:#         top-ring tip  -> arrow points down\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:29:#         bottom-ring   -> arrow points up\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:30:#         left-ring     -> arrow points right\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:31:#         right-ring    -> arrow points left\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:35:#    - Suppose the frame interior is 6x9 and an arrow tip touches the top ring at\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:38:#    - In this combo, the same tip maps to interior start (0,3), but the arrow is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:41:#    - If the arrow uses two colors, the tip color is still the first color and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:49:#    - vertical family: top/bottom arrows create full columns\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:50:#    - horizontal family: left/right arrows create full rows\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:58:#      endpoint is now implicit: it is always the far boundary in the arrow's\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:62:#      components are one-arrow-per-ray with a single startpoint.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:71:#    the cropped interior will be painted, then constructs corner-local bent arrows\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:72:#    outside the frame whose tips project to those starts. Each arrow:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:77:#    - uses a corner-local outer bend so different arrows stay separated\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:82:#    - the external cues are arrows rather than open rectangular frames\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:84:#    The repeated presence of both top and bottom arrows (or left and right arrows)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:90:#    the frame. The outer arrows remain parked in separate corner bands around the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:91:#    frame, so the frame border, startpoints, and arrowheads stay readable.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:94:#    Color behavior remains structural rather than numeric. Monochrome arrows yield\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:95:#    solid rays. Two-color arrows alternate starting from the tip color, so the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:96:#    only color-order cue needed by the solver is directly visible on the arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:97:#    This also makes direction matter: a top arrow and a bottom arrow on different\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:103:#    - original a6 expects two ring touches and therefore misses the arrows\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:104:#    - D3-only would decode the same bent arrow into an adjacent-side L endpoint,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:106:#    The generator therefore always builds bent arrows whose adjacent-side tail\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:223:def _has_arrow_tip(coords: set[tuple[int, int]], tip: tuple[int, int], side: str) -> bool:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:282:    Rebuild full-span interior rays from external bent arrows around a framed rectangle.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:286:    - Useful outer components are bent arrows with exactly one ring-touching tip on the frame.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:292:    - For each valid arrow, map its tip to the corresponding interior boundary cell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:293:    - Starting from that cell, extend a straight row or column in the arrow's direction until the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:295:    - Monochrome arrows paint a solid ray; two-color arrows alternate along the ray beginning with\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:300:      arrow direction determines both the ray orientation and which far side acts as the stop.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:341:        if not _has_arrow_tip(coords, tip, start_side):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:415:def _place_arrow_component(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:473:def _arrow_specs(kind: str, start_side: str, H: int, W: int, r0: int, c0: int, r1: int, c1: int):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:647:        inner, backbone, head = _arrow_specs(kind, start_side, H, W, r0, c0, r1, c1)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a6f40cea/6/code.py:663:        if not _place_arrow_component(grid, comp_mask, backbone, head, colors, bg):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/62593bfd/21/code.py:246:        raise ValueError(\"grid too narrow for crop margins\")\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8b7bacbf/2/code.py:26:#      keeps the visual degree of freedom narrow and repeatable across 2-4 ARC examples.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6ffbe589/6/code.py:74:#      to narrow strips on the middle box / inner box edges.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/71e489b6/25/code.py:234:    blue-region outlier,\" while the marker has narrowed from an unrestricted\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/f931b4a8/34/code.py:33:#     because bbox-based size encoding is capped by quadrant size. The fusion therefore narrows\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/29/code.py:57:    arrow\".\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/29/code.py:96:    color matches and visible arrow orientation.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/29/code.py:249:      color match between a side-mounted legend and its chamber wall; the arrow orientation inside that\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/49/code.py:26:#     The family is intentionally narrow: one basin template, one erasable source component, one\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/11/code.py:9:- The strip-of-frames input format stays the same, but the arrow sprite now uses\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/11/code.py:336:    - Each frame shows one diagonal arrow sprite and the still-unvisited single-pixel markers in the same actor color.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/43/code.py:395:    - The legend no longer means one atomic arrow. It encodes two cardinal sub-moves, and the fixed processing order is part of the rule: horizontal first, vertical second.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/31/code.py:64:  wide enough to be seen by eye, narrow enough that the frame grid still reads as one object.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/18/code.py:35:#    - The basin family is deliberately narrow: two adjacent cups with an optional overflow divider.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/25/code.py:23:#       - then each mover reads the arrow tied to its own color inside that legend;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/25/code.py:25:#         pink stays at its input cell even if its arrow points into open space.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/25/code.py:33:#       the same visible mover color now selects both which arrow to read and how much of that\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/25/code.py:67:#     - Pink is only accepted when its own arrow has at least one open step ahead, so \"pink does\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/25/code.py:257:    Slide multi-colored mover pixels inside sealed wall regions using color-specific arrows and color-specific travel lengths.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/25/code.py:272:    - Wall color chooses which legend applies, while mover color chooses both which arrow to read inside that legend and how much of the available path to traverse.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/25/code.py:353:    raise RuntimeError(\"_sample_legend_dirs: unable to place non-overlapping color-specific arrows\")\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/43/code.py:16:#      The generated landform is a high right-side shelf beside a narrow vertical chute; a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/15/code.py:54:# - Human-facing cue 1: in the rightmost frame the arrow points one way while the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/15/code.py:305:    Key insight: One nearby dot reveals the stride, but the arrow tip no longer\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2b83f449/21/code.py:33:       a left token drops through 6, while under the old narrow-gate rule it would have\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2b83f449/21/code.py:39:     The generator therefore enforces at least two wide gates and at least two narrow gates.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2b83f449/21/code.py:42:     every wide gate as narrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2b83f449/21/code.py:66:     bars, and the corresponding output rows show narrow vs wide opened passages around the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2b83f449/21/code.py:68:   - Because accepted samples must differ from the \"all narrow\" baseline, the width signal is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2b83f449/21/code.py:383:def _baseline_narrow_gates(gates: list[list[tuple[int, int]]]) -> list[list[tuple[int, int]]]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2b83f449/21/code.py:460:    baseline_opened = _build_opened_grid(_baseline_narrow_gates(gates), width)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2b83f449/21/code.py:471:    enriched_stats[\"narrow_gates\"] = int(sum(span == NARROW for row in gates for _center, span in row))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2b83f449/21/code.py:473:    enriched_stats[\"diff_vs_narrow\"] = int(np.count_nonzero(fused_out != baseline_out))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2b83f449/21/code.py:496:    if int(stats[\"narrow_gates\"]) < 2:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2b83f449/21/code.py:506:    if int(stats[\"diff_vs_narrow\"]) < 1:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/26/code.py:78:    - using only cardinal arrows avoids mixing the D6 geometric effect with diagonal corner-cutting\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/26/code.py:88:    - the fused change is inferable from shape geometry plus arrow direction, not from numeric color\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/25/code.py:37:#    The family is deliberately narrow:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/27/code.py:359:    \"\"\"Sample many near-sized areas from a narrow band.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/26/code.py:431:def _add_right_source(g: np.ndarray, top_row: int, narrow_rows: int, color: int) -> None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/26/code.py:474:        narrow_rows = 0\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/26/code.py:482:        _add_right_source(g, right_top, narrow_rows, right_color)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/27/code.py:78:#     visible legend arrows.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/27/code.py:25:# - The geometry is deliberately narrow: always two adjacent basins, always a shorter\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/2b83f449/22/code.py:40:#    - Original assumption: a gate row may have 0-3 gates. The combo narrows this to exactly one\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5545f144/21/code.py:30:#    - The template family stays small and arrow-like: one unique tip plus 1-2\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/8/code.py:17:#    - The fused change fixes the lower area to a long chain of 4 or 5 narrow basins. The top\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/8/code.py:288:    - Bottom-anchored solids form a chain of 4 or 5 narrow basins separated by single divider walls.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/33/code.py:167:      the arrow-like offset inside the legend.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/24/code.py:37:#    - or, for the narrower layout, the same receiver with one inner step.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/10/code.py:38:      \"legend color picks the chamber, legend arrow gives one of four directions, the yellow bar\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/10/code.py:339:    Slide yellow rigid line segments inside closed wall regions using cardinal legend arrows.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/10/code.py:356:    - The legend color still selects which arrow applies to which chamber, but the yellow object is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/1/code.py:90:def _cardinal_arrow_mask(dr: int, dc: int) -> np.ndarray:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/1/code.py:97:    patch[_cardinal_arrow_mask(dr, dc)] = fill\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/1/code.py:101:CARDINAL_TEMPLATE_MASKS = {(dr, dc): _cardinal_arrow_mask(dr, dc) for dr, dc in CARDINAL_DIRS}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/1/code.py:116:def _match_arrow_direction(fill_mask: np.ndarray) -> tuple[int, int] | None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/1/code.py:127:    Detect border legends and decode direction from a dominant-color 5x5 arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/1/code.py:130:    controls. The dominant-color cells form one of four cardinal arrow templates.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/1/code.py:149:        direction = _match_arrow_direction(patch == fill)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/1/code.py:185:    Slide yellow pixels inside walled regions using border legends drawn as cardinal arrows.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/1/code.py:191:      that forms a visible arrow shape, plus a uniform backing color. The wall color C matches the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/1/code.py:196:      find the legend whose dominant arrow color is also C.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/1/code.py:197:    - Read the arrow orientation directly as one of the four cardinal directions.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/1/code.py:202:    from the legend's arrow silhouette itself, and every valid movement is purely horizontal or\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/21897d95/21/code.py:7:#   The old 4-cell T is replaced by a 5-cell arrow. We keep the original logical anchor:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/21897d95/21/code.py:9:#   but the marker now adds one tail cell behind that center. For stem RIGHT, the arrow cells are\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/21897d95/21/code.py:12:#   region the arrow comes out of, read from the tail-side ownership around (r,c-1). The visual\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/21897d95/21/code.py:17:#   arrow-driven recoloring is complete.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/21897d95/21/code.py:21:#   using blue. Here the arrow \">\" does the same recolor first. If the grid also contains a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/21897d95/21/code.py:23:#   the erased arrow-colored patch appears on the opposite side, so a human must not read the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/21897d95/21/code.py:26:#   The arrow keeps only four axial directions, so the rule family does not explode, but the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/21897d95/21/code.py:28:#   5-cell silhouette rather than a 4-cell T. The generator keeps multiple arrows per board and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/21897d95/21/code.py:33:#      That no longer holds for colored arrows: the color-1 part is a 3-cell head cluster plus\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/21897d95/21/code.py:34:#      one singleton tail around a colored center. The solver therefore parses normal arrows from\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/21897d95/21/code.py:35:#      5-cell color-1 components and colored arrows by local geometry around each non-1 center.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/21897d95/21/code.py:36:#   2. The old source lookup from off-marker diagonal cells no longer applies, because the arrow's\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/21897d95/21/code.py:38:#      source from the arrow tail: the marker emerges from that region and points across the border.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/21897d95/21/code.py:40:#      after explicit arrow cells are masked out. This remains safe because arrow cells are marked\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/21897d95/21/code.py:43:#      unflipped arrows, while `main()` applies the L9-triggered flip only afterward.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/21897d95/21/code.py:45:#   The generator reserves every arrow cell plus a neighbor buffer, so normal arrows stay isolated\