ed yellow pixels using the legend arrows, with the touching wall causing the first rebound and later wall hits continuing the reflected path.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/13/code.py:390:    - The yellow pixels are not supposed to reverse-slide directly; they must be fired along the legend arrow from a wall-touching start, so the initial wall contact determines the rebound and the rest of the reflected route.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/71e489b6/36/code.py:26:#    black cells. Example on a narrow black stripe:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8698868d/21/code.py:40:#    - Object count is narrowed to exactly 4 (a 2x2 frame grid). This keeps the legend short and lets frame colors\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/15/code.py:39:#    - The family is intentionally narrow: one validated landform, horizontal mirroring, and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/16/code.py:24:#      original: two differently colored fenced rooms might use two different arrows, and the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/40/code.py:46:  - Room interiors are narrow in the stacking axis and longer in the motion axis, which\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/39/code.py:43:    - B-only rooms no longer follow B's shown arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/39/code.py:65:      with three colors and a centroid-based arrow cue.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/39/code.py:87:  - The A-only room anchors the normal meaning of a legend arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/39/code.py:88:  - The B-only room shows that B's displayed arrow is not the real motion cue.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/39/code.py:109:      uses B's displayed arrow directly instead of sign(disp_A - disp_B).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/39/code.py:342:    two-legend rule instead of a one-color one-arrow lookup.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/39/code.py:353:    - A's true direction is the arrow shown inside A.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/39/code.py:354:    - B's true direction is not its own shown arrow; it is sign(A_display - B_display).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/39/code.py:361:    legend directions must itself be decoded from the relationship between the two visible arrows.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3a25b0d8/30/code.py:28:  colors, so color only narrows the search to a group; it does not identify the true `dst`\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:17:      drawn as a coarse arrow silhouette.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:18:    - The visible arrow direction is the legend's displayed vector.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:22:    - Suppose the left legend uses color 7 and shows a right arrow, so display_left = (0, 1).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:23:    - Suppose the right legend uses color 5 and shows a down arrow, so display_right = (1, 0).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:24:    - In the source task or in the pure arrow-shape variant, color-5 rooms would slide down.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:28:        2. decode each rectangle's arrow shape into a displayed vector;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:37:  - The arrow silhouettes are intentionally chunky and asymmetric, so humans can read them\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:55:    3. the dominant legend-color mask matches one of the 5x5/6x7 arrow templates;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:65:  - Humans can first read the left arrow in the usual way, then compare it to the right arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:66:  - Right-colored rooms visibly disobey their own displayed arrow and instead follow the vector\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:79:  - Color 1 is just a legend plate color; all nontrivial inference comes from arrow orientation\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:83:  - Metric: compare the combo output against a legacy-arrow output that incorrectly lets the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:84:    second wall color follow its own displayed arrow directly.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:90:  - main() assumes the dominant legend color is the wall color; generator uses masks where the arrow\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:171:def _cardinal_arrow_mask(dr: int, dc: int) -> np.ndarray:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:176:def _diagonal_arrow_mask(dr: int, dc: int) -> np.ndarray:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:188:CARDINAL_TEMPLATE_MASKS = {(dr, dc): _cardinal_arrow_mask(dr, dc) for dr, dc in CARDINAL_DIRS}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:189:DIAGONAL_TEMPLATE_MASKS = {(dr, dc): _diagonal_arrow_mask(dr, dc) for dr, dc in DIAGONAL_DIRS}\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:194:    patch[_cardinal_arrow_mask(dr, dc)] = fill\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:200:    patch[_diagonal_arrow_mask(dr, dc)] = fill\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:210:def _match_arrow_direction(fill_mask: np.ndarray) -> tuple[int, int] | None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:249:    direction = _match_arrow_direction(patch == fill_col)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:362:    Slide yellow pixels inside enclosed rooms using arrow legends plus a derived second instruction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:366:    - Two small legend rectangles sit on the top border. Each uses wall color C as a chunky arrow silhouette on a color-1 plate.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:370:    - Decode each legend's displayed direction from the orientation of its wall-colored arrow shape.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88e364bc/20/code.py:375:    first read arrow shapes as vectors, then derive the second color's true motion by vector subtraction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/4/code.py:51:    column), which keeps the visual language narrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/44/code.py:41:#    - Original code accepted any grid size. This combo intentionally narrows the family to\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/16/code.py:21:#        forces the frontier to progress through a narrower lower path;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/9/code.py:16:#     The local water physics stays the same as the original task, but the admissible input family is narrowed to a\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a32d8b75/26/code.py:644:        narrowed = [c for c in subset if score_fn(c) == best]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a32d8b75/26/code.py:645:        if narrowed:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a32d8b75/26/code.py:646:            subset = narrowed\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/8e5c0c38/22/code.py:191:    - Process foreground colors in priority order by compactness: clean the narrow reference color first by the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/19/code.py:320:    - The lower structure is one bottom-anchored chain of 4-5 narrow basins, while the input water starts\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/24/code.py:43:#    The design is intentionally narrow instead of fully free-form:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/12/code.py:31:#    The layout family is intentionally narrow: three cups, one lower source roof, and only five\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/48/code.py:16:#      a pair of narrow lower shafts that can catch falling liquid. The fused palette is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/48/code.py:33:#    - The color freedom is intentionally narrow. Using exactly two fresh colors plus one old\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/48/code.py:36:#    - The geometry is also narrow: both fresh sources feed into two one-cell-wide shafts.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/28a6681f/48/code.py:71:#      narrow liquid shafts. No legends or overlapping annotations are added.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/a32d8b75/28/code.py:67:#    - The legend still uses one narrow column block per side, so symbols remain easy\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/25/code.py:12:  sprites are forced into a very narrow outer ring).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/25/code.py:37:- The sprite band is intentionally narrow, but not so narrow that generation becomes impossible. A fixed\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/25/code.py:41:  That gives enough variety for matching while avoiding impossible 3x3-like sprites in the narrow ring.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/25/code.py:51:  assumption, so sprite generation must use a narrower shape library and lower sprite counts.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/25/code.py:92:  2. The outside sprite region is only the narrow perimeter ring around that large frame.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/25/code.py:282:# Sprite scattering in the narrow edge ring\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/67e490f4/25/code.py:464:    \"\"\"Try to place one sprite in the narrow edge ring.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/c7f57c3e/8/code.py:69:#     narrower rectangle.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/88bcf3b4/15/code.py:43:#   - The long-approach change is kept in a narrow range: obstacle md 5-12, mostly produced by\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/faa9f03d/23/code.py:28:#    The family is kept narrow on purpose: a vertical backbone crosses two horizontal lines, which is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e453dd6/30/code.py:350:    assert flush_c0 >= 0, \"left pane too narrow for 3x3 sprites\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e453dd6/12/code.py:35:#      narrow (2-4 rows) just like the original right receiving pane stayed narrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/6e453dd6/24/code.py:72:#     - each generated sprite has nozzle width 1 or 2, both visually legible in a narrow left pane\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:28:#   3. Reflection is read from arrow glyphs inside the matched target quadrant:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:29:#      right-arrow => LR flip, down-arrow => UD flip. Quadrant position no longer\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:35:#     * connector + only down-arrow -> copy there, but apply UD instead of LR;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:36:#     * connector + both arrows -> copy there with both flips.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:59:#   arrows are instruction artifacts. They are stripped before motifs are stamped.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:66:#   regions are large enough for anchors, arrow glyphs, and at least one-cell\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:70:# - Active target anchors reserve a top band for arrows and at least one extra row\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:77:#   3. read the arrows in those same quadrants to decide the reflection bits.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:83:# - Colors are function-separated: divider=1, anchors=2, arrows=3, bg=4,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:97:#   quadrants with both a connector color repeated in TL and at least one arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:330:    Copy the TL motif into connector-matched regions, scaling by anchor size and mirroring by arrow markers.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:335:    - Each active target shares a repeated connector color with a TL-side connector stub, and that same target region also contains one or two green arrow glyphs.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:339:    - Extract the TL motif while ignoring connector colors, then for each active target read its arrows: right-arrow means LR reflection, down-arrow means UD reflection.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:342:    Key insight: The divider only tells you where the four regions are; connector colors decide which anchors participate at all, and arrow glyphs independently decide how each matched copy is reflected.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:561:def _paint_arrow(grid: np.ndarray, top: int, left: int, shape: frozenset[tuple[int, int]]) -> None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:569:        _paint_arrow(grid, q_r0 + 1, q_c0 + 1, RIGHT_ARROW)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/26/code.py:571:        _paint_arrow(grid, q_r0 + 1, q_c0 + 5, DOWN_ARROW)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/65b59efc/29/code.py:64:    Key insight: The separator spacing reveals the slot-grid dimensions, but the occupied extent of the top-band shapes reveals the narrower template width; marker locality and marker color must be decoded separately.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/29/code.py:134:    \"\"\"Return border particles that have exactly one adjacent arrow marker.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/29/code.py:193:    \"\"\"Move magenta edge particles by following explicit arrows through spiral corridors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/9bbf930d/29/code.py:196:    - bg=7, particle=6, arrow=2.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ce