5cfb/15/code.py:558:        if any(cell in occupied or cell in drawn or cell in pair_arrow_cells for cell in ray_cells):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/15/code.py:563:        pair_blue_cells = blue_occupied | pair_arrow_cells\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/15/code.py:564:        forbidden = occupied | drawn | path_cells | ray_cells | pair_arrow_cells\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/15/code.py:577:            \"arrow_cells\": (p_cells, q_cells),\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/15/code.py:610:        p_cells, q_cells = pair[\"arrow_cells\"]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/15/code.py:632:        p_cells, q_cells = pair[\"arrow_cells\"]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:26:#   1. one green arrow glyph placed near the target anchor,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:28:# - The arrow gives the mandatory \"primary\" reflection:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:29:#     right arrow  -> LR reflection is required,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:30:#     down arrow   -> UD reflection is required.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:32:#   also needed. Concretely, after reading the arrow, the solver compares the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:38:#     1. read the local arrow; if it is a down arrow, start from UD instead,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:42:#   Difference: quadrant position no longer selects the mirror; the arrow gives\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:46:# - The arrow vocabulary stays tiny: exactly two 3x3 glyphs.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:49:#   arrows alone are insufficient, and anchors alone are ambiguous without first\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:50:#   knowing which primary axis the arrow selected.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:60:#   must remove arrow markers before stamping or they would leak into the output.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:62:#   arrows exist only outside TL, and the TL motif remains one 4-connected\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:66:# - The right arrow is drawn 1 cell above the anchor; the down arrow is drawn 1\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:71:# - The arrow is kept disconnected from the anchor, so component extraction for\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:75:# - A human can read the arrow as the obvious first instruction, then notice\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:76:#   that two targets with the same arrow can still differ because of the anchor\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:78:# - The generator enforces both arrow types, both anchor states, and at least\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:82:# - Green is reserved for arrows and never used inside the motif.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:84:#   anchor, while arrows sit outside with a one-cell gap. This avoids overlap and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:88:# - No hidden color ordering is used. Red still marks anchors, green means arrow\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:91:#   \"arrows only\" or \"original quadrant rule\" at the whole-output level.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:95:# - Every non-TL quadrant has exactly one arrow glyph and exactly one decorated\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:97:# - Each target anchor matches exactly one of two templates once its arrow-chosen\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:212:def _read_arrow_primaries(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:225:        assert key != (0, 0), \"arrows are only allowed in non-TL quadrants\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:226:        assert key not in primaries, \"each non-TL quadrant must contain exactly one arrow glyph\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:229:            raise AssertionError(\"unrecognized arrow glyph\")\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:233:        assert key in primaries, \"each non-TL quadrant must contain exactly one arrow glyph\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:254:    Replicate the TL motif across quadrants with size scaling, arrow-selected primary reflection, and anchor-selected extra reflection.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:259:    - Each non-TL quadrant contains one decorated red anchor plus exactly one nearby green arrow glyph; the anchor size still varies by quadrant.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:263:    - In each target quadrant, read the arrow first to get the mandatory reflection axis: right arrow means LR, down arrow means UD.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:264:    - Compare the observed target-anchor template against the TL anchor under either that primary flip alone or both flips; apply the matching transform to the full source motif, scale by anchor-size ratio, align the red region to the target anchor, and stamp non-background pixels. Remove the green arrows from the final output.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:266:    Key insight: The arrow does not fully specify the transform by itself. It only chooses the primary axis; the anchor's internal asymmetry tells you whether an additional orthogonal flip must be composed on top of it.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:275:    primaries, marker_components = _read_arrow_primaries(g, mid_r, mid_c)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:515:def _paint_arrow(grid: np.ndarray, top: int, left: int, shape: frozenset[tuple[int, int]]) -> None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:520:def _place_arrow(grid: np.ndarray, anchor_r: int, anchor_c: int, primary: tuple[bool, bool]) -> None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:523:        _paint_arrow(grid, anchor_r - 4, anchor_c, shape)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:525:        _paint_arrow(grid, anchor_r, anchor_c - 4, shape)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:611:    arrow_only_out = _build_output(patch, anchor_patch, tl_r0, tl_c0, target_sizes, anchors, primaries)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:613:    if int(np.count_nonzero(out != arrow_only_out)) < MIN_VARIANT_DIFF:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:624:        _place_arrow(inp, ar, ac, primaries[key])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/b10624e5/19/code.py:627:    # it is the reason arrow_only_out differs from out.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/e12f9a14/43/code.py:68:#       combo outputs differ from a narrow unbounded baseline on the same inputs.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/898e7135/53/code.py:126:    assert w >= 2, \"grid too narrow for color legend\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/3dc255db/30/code.py:32:#      canvases, so the count range is intentionally narrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/dd6b8c4b/33/code.py:248:    - A unique red cell marks the middle of a green 3x3 destination block, while maroon pixels sit on narrow floor corridors or in isolated pockets outside it.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/8f3a5a89/D15/2/code.py:35:#    - The variant is kept narrow: only the marker identification and flood source\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/136b0064/25/code.py:95:#   long-left area-6 branch, so the walk grammar does not collapse to a narrow\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/136b0064/26/code.py:17:     Each strip shows one glyph sample on the left and one 5x5 arrow exemplar on the right:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/136b0064/26/code.py:71:   - The 5x11 legend strips use a blank spacer column between glyph sample and arrow sample, keeping\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/136b0064/26/code.py:82:   - Legend colors are purely stylistic: all legend shapes share one color, while arrow colors only\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/136b0064/26/code.py:83:     help distinguish arrow exemplars visually.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/136b0064/26/code.py:89:     shape-vs-arrow samples; generator uses exactly that layout.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/136b0064/26/code.py:134:# Right-side legend layout: one 5x5 glyph sample + spacer + one 5x5 arrow sample.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/136b0064/26/code.py:272:    raise AssertionError(f\"unrecognized legend arrow patch:\\n{mask}\")\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/136b0064/26/code.py:281:        arrow_patch = right[r0 : r0 + 5, LEGEND_ARROW_C0 : LEGEND_ARROW_C0 + 5]\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/136b0064/26/code.py:285:        glyph_to_dir[glyph_id] = _decode_dir_patch(arrow_patch, bg)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/136b0064/26/code.py:307:      shows a glyph sample and a 5x5 arrow sample.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/136b0064/26/code.py:542:        arrow = np.asarray(_DIR_TEMPLATES[direction], dtype=int)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/136b0064/26/code.py:546:        ] = arrow * int(LEGEND_ARROW_COLORS[direction])\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/29/code.py:185:def _narrow_vertical_corridor(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/29/code.py:432:        if not _narrow_vertical_corridor(grid, jc, top1, jr, child_n, min_open_steps=5):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/29/code.py:435:        if not _narrow_vertical_corridor(grid, right_root[1], top2, jr, child_n, min_open_steps=6):\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/18/code.py:34:#      target. If a choke narrows below 6, the mover stops at the farthest\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/18/code.py:74:#    - Big mover sizes 5..7 make narrow gates visibly narrower than the mover.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/18/code.py:84:#      large mover footprint interacting with visibly narrow chokepoints.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/18/code.py:308:    narrow_w = random.randint(1, max(1, n - 2))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/18/code.py:309:    remove = int(n - narrow_w)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:21:#   - Every sprite is now a 3-pixel arrow that points directly along one of the 4 cardinal\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:24:#     encoded by arrow family:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:25:#       * kind \"L\": skew hook arrow, range 4\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:26:#       * kind \"V\": symmetric chevron arrow, range 6\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:29:#       * fused analogue can be a short-range up arrow {(5,5),(6,4),(6,5)}.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:31:#       * a long-range up arrow {(5,5),(6,4),(6,6)} points the same way but lands at\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:32:#         (-1,5) conceptually, so the generator only places such arrows where tip+6*dir stays\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:34:#         arrow silhouette plus the induced landing distance.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:36:#   - The fusion is not simplified into a single arrow template: there are two reusable shape\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:41:#   - Generator examples force both arrow families to appear and reject cases whose output is\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:45:#     monochrome, 8-connected arrow matching exactly one template. The generator uses only\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:46:#     those templates and validates them with _arrow_info(...).\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:50:#     _arrow_info returns only those labels, and generator placements are precomputed by kind.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:54:#   - Placements are precomputed only when both the arrow cells and tip+range*dir destination\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:59:#   - All arrows are axis-aligned, so the D1 change is visible directly in the input.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:60:#   - The two arrow silhouettes are visually distinct, and both appear repeatedly, so the D2\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:66:#   - No rule depends on hidden ordering or color ranking; only local shape family, arrow tip,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:111:def _arrow_info(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:114:    \"\"\"Return (tip_rc, dir_rc, kind) for a 3-cell axis arrow sprite, else None.\"\"\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:147:        info = _arrow_info(comp)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:177:    Fire straight projectiles from 3-pixel arrow sprites, with range determined by arrow family.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:180:    - The background is the most common color, and each foreground component is a 3-cell monochrome arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:181:    - Every arrow points directly up, down, left, or right from a single tip cell.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:182:    - There are two arrow silhouettes: a skew hook family and a symmetric chevron family.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:185:    - Read the firing direction from the arrow tip itself; all projectiles travel along cardinal rays only.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:186:    - The arrow family sets the distance: hook arrows (the former L-coded family) travel 4 cells, while chevron arrows (the former V-coded family) travel 6 cells.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:191:    - Direction has become visually explicit, so the remaining inference is to separate the two arrow families and notice that they keep different fixed ranges even though they shoot along the same four axes.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/409aa875/8/code.py:319:    return all(_arrow_info(comp) is not None for comp in comps)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/5dbc8537/19/code.py:44:#      narrows the target to the two blue-striped holes. One hole shows a red\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/25/code.py:36:#      n×n clearance test needed for gradual narrowing.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/25/code.py:39:#      because the taper can create upstream slack before the true narrowing point.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/25/code.py:48:#    - The river remains pixel-connected in blocked cases because the narrow spine still links\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/25/code.py:51:#    - Humans can see the one-sided staircase narrowing near the target.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/332f06d7/25/code.py:53:#      the narrowing; it stops noticeably earlier, roughly halfway along the usable route.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:18:#    - In this combo, each active source endpoint becomes a 3-cell blue L-arrow. The degree-2\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:19:#      corner is the tip; the missing quadrant is the arrow's forward diagonal direction.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:20:#    - Each arrow also has exactly one orthogonally adjacent marker cell in a non-blue,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:21:#      non-magenta color. Marker colors are the pairing labels: two arrows with the same marker\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:22:#      color form one active pair. The segment is drawn between their tips, and both arrows must\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:24:#    - Example: suppose arrows A and B have red markers. A's tip is at (3,3) and points SE;\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:26:#      inside the arrow bodies and could not tell which blue cells are endpoints. New logic first\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:27:#      parses each 4-connected 3-cell L, extracts its tip/direction, groups arrows by marker\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:31:#      arrow distractor on the same diagonal extension as a real pair. Geometry-only pairing would\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:40:#    - At most one uniquely marked distractor arrow is added. That is enough to make marker-based\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:44:#    - The old `by_color[line_color]` pool is invalid because a single arrow contains three blue\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:51:#      3-cell L arrow, each such arrow must have exactly one adjacent marker, active marker colors\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:52:#      must appear on exactly two arrows, and those two arrows must face each other along a shared\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:59:#      distractor arrows.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:60:#    - Marker cells are attached to the tip side of an arrow, stay off all active diagonals and\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:61:#      future rays, and touch only their own arrow in 4-neighborhood.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:63:#      arrow unambiguously.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:66:#    - Humans can directly see the L-arrow cue and infer that the diagonal leaves the tip through\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:68:#    - Repeated marker colors across distant arrows make the pairing label visible in a few\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:71:#      then connect arrow tips\" is more plausible than any accidental one-off geometry heuristic.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:76:#    - Markers sit beside arrow tips instead of on the diagonal itself, so arrows, labels, path\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:78:#    - Distractor arrows, when present, are placed on diagonal extensions outside the active\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:82:#    - Marker colors do not transform into output colors; they only label which arrows belong\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:123:def _arrow_cells(tip: tuple[int, int], direction: tuple[int, int]) -> set[tuple[int, int]]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:157:def _find_arrows(grid: np.ndarray, bg: int) -> list[dict]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:160:    arrows: list[dict] = []\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:206:            assert len(marker_cells) == 1, \"each arrow must have exactly one adjacent marker\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:208:            arrows.append(\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:218:    arrows.sort(key=lambda a: (a[\"tip\"], a[\"marker_color\"]))\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:219:    return arrows\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:235:def _active_pairs_from_arrows(arrows: list[dict]) -> list[dict]:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:237:    for arrow in arrows:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:238:        marker_groups.setdefault(arrow[\"marker_color\"], []).append(arrow)\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:248:        assert ordered is not None, \"same-color arrow pair must be diagonal and face inward\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:265:    Read marked blue arrow sprites as diagonal endpoints, connect same-marked arrow pairs, then emit rays from magenta cells on those diagonals.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:268:    - bg = most common color. Blue source objects are 3-cell L-shaped arrows whose corner cell is the tip and whose missing quadrant gives the forward diagonal.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:269:    - Each arrow has one orthogonally adjacent marker cell in a non-blue, non-magenta color. Marker colors that appear twice define the active arrow pairs.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:270:    - Magenta trigger cells may lie on the interior of active diagonals; uniquely marked arrows or off-diagonal magenta cells can appear as distractors.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:273:    - Parse every valid blue arrow, group arrows by marker color, and for each marker color that labels exactly two arrows, draw the blue diagonal segment between their tips.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code_combo/db695cfb/6/code.py:277:    - Neither raw blue geometry nor marker colors alone are sufficient: the L-shape identifies the true tip and diagonal direction, while the repeated marker color decides which two arrows should actually be connected.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/