onally small: only two 3x3 arrow glyphs exist,\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:48:#   anchors. With markers added, leaving `out` unchanged would leak arrows into the\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:55:# - Marker arrows are placed relative to each target anchor with a one-cell gap:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:56:#   the LR arrow sits above the anchor, the UD arrow sits left of the anchor.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:80:# - Each non-TL quadrant has one or two green arrow components and at least one arrow.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:82:# - Each arrow glyph matches one of the two exact templates below.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:203:        assert grouped[key] >= 1, \"each non-TL quadrant must contain at least one arrow marker\"\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:210:    Copy the TL motif into the other quadrants, scaling by anchor size and mirroring by arrow markers.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:215:    - Each non-TL quadrant additionally contains one or two green arrow glyphs: a right arrow requests LR reflection, and a down arrow requests UD reflection.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:219:    - For each target quadrant, read its arrow glyphs to decide which reflections to apply, then scale the transformed motif by the ratio between target-anchor size and TL-anchor size.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:222:    Key insight: The red anchor still tells you the scale, but mirror behavior is no longer implied by quadrant position; it is written explicitly by the nearby arrow symbols.\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py:443:def _paint_arrow(grid: np.ndarray, top: int, left: int, shape: frozenset[tuple[int, int]]) -> None:\n/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ceshi/code/b10624e5/D12/1/code.py: